We are given that points $A$ and $B$ lie on the graph of $y = \log_{2} x$. This means that the $y$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are both logarithms with base $2$ of some positive real number $x$. Since the $y$-coordinate of the midpoint of $AB$ is $2$, we can use the midpoint formula to write the following equation:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\log_{2} x_{A} + \log_{2} x_{B}}{2} & = 2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \log_{2} x_{A} + \log_{2} x_{B} & = 4.
\end{align*}
We can use the product property of logarithms to simplify this equation:
\begin{align*}
\log_{2} x_{A} + \log_{2} x_{B} & = \log_{2} (x_{A} \cdot x_{B})\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \log_{2} (x_{A} \cdot x_{B}) & = 4.
\end{align*}
This means that $x_{A} \cdot x_{B} = 2^{4} = 16.$ We can write $16$ as a product of two positive integers in the following way:
\begin{align*}
16 & = 16 \cdot 1\\
& = 8 \cdot 2\\
& = 4 \cdot 4.
\end{align*}
Each of these pairs of positive integers corresponds to the $x$-coordinates of two points on the graph of $y = \log_{2} x$. The positive difference between the $x$-coordinates of each pair is $\boxed{8}$.
